## Baron's approach one turn after her entry, and the dismissal when she
## produces the letter or speaks to him. The mercenaries are barred
## offstage and heard (gap 6); the Queen's Guard is a line (gap 7); she
## keeps the letter (gap 5).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase confrontation-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — the confrontation: Baron Fossville comes
  through the crowd to her, early, before anyone else has spoken; the court
  turns to watch; the Queen is in the room. What he says to her, and what
  he demands, are David's. Fossville does NOT see her — he knows from the
  letter.)
end phrase

define phrase dismissal-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — the dismissal: the letter produced before the
  court, the Duke's own hand naming Jacqueline; the Queen, who already knows
  what Fossville did, threatens him quietly and lets him go; he tries to
  bring his mercenaries in and the Queen's Guard bars them at the doors,
  heard, not seen; and the room opens to her — the Queen on her throne, and
  beside it Bobby, alive, and Pieter. Nothing is burned and nothing is
  confessed aloud. The lines are David's.)
end phrase

## The Baron at the ball (`story.ni:9653`, the `otherwise` branch), with
## its first-time tail; his block is in `gallows.chord`.

define phrase baron-at-the-ball, first-time
  Now that you're up close, the cracks in Fossville's façade show themselves
  clearly. His thick, black hair is disheveled. His face is haggard. He's
  tried to hide the lines and the dark circles under his eyes with extra
  make-up, but it just makes him look more ghoulish. With some
  satisfaction, you realize that you must have been really running the
  Baron ragged the last couple of days. No wonder he hasn't made an
  appearance at his own party.
or
  Now that you're up close, the cracks in Fossville's façade show themselves
  clearly. His thick, black hair is disheveled. His face is haggard. He's
  tried to hide the lines and the dark circles under his eyes with extra
  make-up, but it just makes him look more ghoulish.
end phrase

## His platform line (`story.ni:9660`) is the square's marker now, since a
## presence line cannot vary (GH #364) and he stands here too.

define phrase fossville-on-the-platform, stopping while Baron Fossville is in the Lord's Market
  Baron Fossville stands on the platform, addressing the crowd.
end phrase

